{
  "summary": "The original plan's reliance on Textual's live reload is misguided — Textual's --dev mode only hot-reloads CSS, not Python code. For true Python code reload, there are three viable approaches: (1) A supervisor/wrapper pattern where the TUI process is managed by a lightweight parent process that watches pipeline/*.py with watchdog and restarts the TUI on changes — this is the safest and most reliable approach since it avoids stale module state. (2) Using importlib.reload() to hot-reload changed modules at runtime — this is fragile because it doesn't update existing class instances, can leave stale references, and the TUI's 4000-line monolith with reactive state makes partial reload extremely risky. (3) Extending the existing _check_for_changes() 10-second polling loop to also monitor pipeline/*.py mtimes and trigger a full app restart via os.execv() (re-exec the same process) — this is the simplest implementation since the polling infrastructure already exists, avoids adding watchdog as a dependency, and a clean self-restart preserves the user's intent while guaranteeing fresh module state. Approach 3 (self-restart via os.execv in the existing polling loop) is recommended as the pragmatic choice: minimal code change, no new dependencies, and guaranteed correctness. The TUI could save minimal state (active board, selected feature) to a temp file before restart to preserve user context."
}
